WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.534
-The new show is called
"The Old Man and the Pool."

00:00:03.534 --> 00:00:07.801
I was lucky to see
one of the first previews.

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:11.067
-Yeah, yeah.
It's all a work in progress.

00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:15.501
I'm going to Berkeley
in January for three weeks.

00:00:15.501 --> 00:00:18.267
It's like, you know,
the first stretch of shows.

00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:21.601
I'm doing 21 performances
in January.

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:22.934
I'm really excited.
-That's wild.

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:25.000
-Yeah, it's wild, right?
-Yeah, you could just relocate.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.701
That's going to be
the first time really doing this

00:00:27.701 --> 00:00:29.334
since COVID and all this.

00:00:29.334 --> 00:00:31.067
Are you excited for this?

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:32.133
-I...

00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:34.701
My sister Patty,
lives in the Bay Area,

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:35.968
and so it was actually
the first place,

00:00:35.968 --> 00:00:37.567
when I started out
as a road comic,

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:39.367
that I wanted to perform
was in the Bay Area

00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:41.467
so I could visit
my sister Patty.

00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:43.300
Just like,
a sweet thing, you know?

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.601
And the problem was
getting booked there.

00:00:45.601 --> 00:00:46.801
When you're starting out,

00:00:46.801 --> 00:00:48.701
it's that there's tons
of great local comics,

00:00:48.701 --> 00:00:50.767
and so they don't really
want you.

00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:52.601
Generally, they're like,
"We're good."

00:00:52.601 --> 00:00:54.234
And I'm like, "Yeah, I'll come."

00:00:54.234 --> 00:00:55.667
They're like, "No, no."
-"We're fine."

00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:57.868
-"Yeah, no, we have comedians,"
you know?

00:00:57.868 --> 00:01:01.501
Because I was starting out
as an opener,

00:01:01.501 --> 00:01:05.200
and this guy Jeff
who owns a club, he goes like,

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:06.801
"You can work for a week
in San Francisco,

00:01:06.801 --> 00:01:09.267
but then you have to work for
my club for a week

00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:10.834
in Sacramento with no hotel."

00:01:10.834 --> 00:01:12.701
Which, in show business,
it's like

00:01:12.701 --> 00:01:15.167
kind of hard
to not have a hotel,

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:17.534
because you don't live
in Sacramento.

00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:19.367
Like,
I don't live in Sacramento.

00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:20.701
-How are you going to do that?
-What am I going to do?

00:01:20.701 --> 00:01:23.901
So I stayed on my sister
Patty's couch in San Francisco,

00:01:23.901 --> 00:01:27.033
and then I commuted
100 miles every day

00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:30.000
to Sacramento for a week.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.067
-That's commitment, right there.
-Right.

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:34.734
But the reason why
I really remember it

00:01:34.734 --> 00:01:39.601
is that I was backstage
at the Sacramento Punch Line

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:41.300
and someone offered me
marijuana,

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.667
and I don't use substances
before I perform.

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:45.133
I never had.

00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:48.334
And I go, "What's the worst
thing that can happen?"

00:01:48.334 --> 00:01:50.267
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:52.801
And I'll tell you what it was.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.667
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.667 --> 00:02:00.767
So what it was is,
I'm like a person who,

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:02.567
when you smoke pot,
I'm like the least fun person

00:02:02.567 --> 00:02:03.901
to smoke pot with.

00:02:03.901 --> 00:02:05.133
I'm the I'm the guy
who's always like,

00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:08.534
"Do you guys hate me?
Why does my heart hurt?

00:02:08.534 --> 00:02:10.868
Do I have rickets?"
I'm that guy.

00:02:10.868 --> 00:02:15.601
And so I took one drag,
one drag,

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:16.567
walked out on stage.

00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:22.567
I forgot my act, forgot my name,

00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:24.934
and I forgot
that I was a comedian.

00:02:24.934 --> 00:02:26.667
-Wow.

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:28.234
-I'll act it out
if the cameras can follow.

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:29.534
[ Light applause ]
So I'm up there --

00:02:29.534 --> 00:02:31.167
Thank you so much.

00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:33.167
Yeah, yeah, actually.
No, that's good.

00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:35.601
-Should introduce you?
-"Please welcome..." Yeah, yeah.

00:02:35.601 --> 00:02:38.267
-Please welcome Mike Birbiglia!

00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:39.968
[ Applause ]

00:02:39.968 --> 00:02:42.701
-I'll do it --
can I do it over here?

00:02:42.701 --> 00:02:43.801
[ Drumroll ]

00:02:43.801 --> 00:02:48.100
-Please welcome Mike Birbiglia!

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:52.334 --> 00:02:53.934
-Hey.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.267
[ Laughter ]

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:04.133
For 30 seconds. For 30 seconds.

00:03:04.133 --> 00:03:06.834
Hey.

00:03:06.834 --> 00:03:09.534
Please welcome
your headliner, Tom Rowe.

00:03:09.534 --> 00:03:12.501
-Oh, my God!
-No act. No act!

00:03:12.501 --> 00:03:16.100
I had no jokes. I told no jokes.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:17.501
I was supposed to be on
for 20 minutes.

00:03:17.501 --> 00:03:19.801
I did 30 seconds
of standing there,

00:03:19.801 --> 00:03:22.167
and I never used a substance

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:24.434
before walking
onstage ever again.

00:03:24.434 --> 00:03:27.100
-Yeah, that's a good lesson
to learn.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.267
-And then I stayed
at a motel that night.

00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:32.334
I used my paycheck
from the entire week

00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:34.100
to put myself up
in a Sacramento hotel.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.033
-Oh, my God.
-I know.

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:37.567
-Where are you staying now?

00:03:37.567 --> 00:03:39.400
-I'm staying -- Oh, man.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.200
Last time I went to Berkeley,

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.167
the last show I did,
which was called "The New One,"

00:03:43.167 --> 00:03:45.033
which was across
the street on Broadway.

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:46.934
-I loved "The New One."
-Thanks.

00:03:46.934 --> 00:03:50.067
And it's so nostalgic
to be here.

00:03:50.067 --> 00:03:51.534
Yeah, because it was
right around Christmas,

00:03:51.534 --> 00:03:52.734
like three years ago,

00:03:52.734 --> 00:03:54.934
and I did it at
Berkeley Repertory Theater,

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:57.667
which is where I'm going,
and I stayed in an Airbnb,

00:03:57.667 --> 00:03:59.868
which I find to be
a dishonest acronym.

00:03:59.868 --> 00:04:01.133
No breakfast.

00:04:03.434 --> 00:04:06.934
Still waiting on the breakfast.
-Bed, sure, yeah.

00:04:06.934 --> 00:04:12.300
-"AirB and we live nearby."

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:15.901
We stayed at an Airbnb.

00:04:15.901 --> 00:04:18.868
It was my wife,
Jen and our daughter Oona,

00:04:18.868 --> 00:04:20.167
who was three at the time.

00:04:20.167 --> 00:04:23.367
We loved it. It was like,
this pristine place.

00:04:23.367 --> 00:04:24.634
But then we realized
for the first time

00:04:24.634 --> 00:04:27.067
that we're not pristine people.

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:28.534
-No.
-I think we're messy people,

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:31.667
because afterwards,
we messaged them, we're like,

00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:36.167
"Love to stay here again,"
the owners of the BNB,

00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:41.467
and they were like, "You should
visit the city again."

00:04:41.467 --> 00:04:44.434
I was like, "At your apartment,"

00:04:44.434 --> 00:04:46.968
and they were like,
"We wish you well in your life."

00:04:46.968 --> 00:04:48.167
You know what I mean?

00:04:48.167 --> 00:04:49.968
-Wish you well in you life.

00:04:49.968 --> 00:04:53.067
-I was like, "We wish our life
was at your apartment."

00:04:53.067 --> 00:04:57.167
And they were like, "We wish to
never see you again."

00:04:57.167 --> 00:04:58.534
So this time,
I'm staying at a hotel.

00:04:58.534 --> 00:05:00.133
-You're doing a hotel?
-Yeah.

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:04.167
In Sacramento, I mean.
I'm commuting.

00:05:04.167 --> 00:05:06.334
-To Berkeley all the way
from Sacramento.

00:05:06.334 --> 00:05:07.667
You got to -- [Snaps fingers]

00:05:07.667 --> 00:05:09.067
You can't get too big.

00:05:09.067 --> 00:05:11.133
You got to take yourself down.
You got to check yourself.

00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:13.000
-Yeah. Yeah.
-Before you wreck yourself.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.667
-Yeah, absolutely. Yeah.

00:05:14.667 --> 00:05:17.033
-I want to talk about your
podcast, too, "Working it Out,"

00:05:17.033 --> 00:05:19.834
which is like --
I listen to maybe two podcasts,.

00:05:19.834 --> 00:05:21.601
-Which ones?

00:05:21.601 --> 00:05:25.234
-"Working it Out" is one.
-"Working it Out."

00:05:25.234 --> 00:05:27.901
-"SmartLess," maybe.
-"SmartLess" is so good.

00:05:27.901 --> 00:05:29.601
You were great on "SmartLess."

00:05:29.601 --> 00:05:31.434
And I want you to
come on my podcast.

00:05:31.434 --> 00:05:32.834
I keep texting you.

00:05:32.834 --> 00:05:33.901
I think, do I have
the right number?

00:05:33.901 --> 00:05:38.701
Is it 646...
-Okay.

00:05:38.701 --> 00:05:40.501
-You want Jimmy to be
on the podcast or not?

00:05:40.501 --> 00:05:41.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:41.667 --> 00:05:43.734
You want Jimmy to be
on the podcast or not?

00:05:43.734 --> 00:05:46.334
646...
-Don't give out my number!

00:05:46.334 --> 00:05:47.801
-No, no, this is how it works,
right?

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:50.634
You come on "The Tonight Show."
-Yeah.

00:05:50.634 --> 00:05:53.567
-And then you get the phone
number, and then you text...

00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:54.901
-That's close to my number,
right there.

00:05:54.901 --> 00:05:58.501
-646. Go ahead.
-No.

00:05:58.501 --> 00:06:01.400
You have my number.
-Next.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:02.801
-You have my number.
We've texted.

00:06:02.801 --> 00:06:04.834
-Alright.
-I love your podcast,

00:06:04.834 --> 00:06:07.334
because I haven't done
standup in such a long time.

00:06:07.334 --> 00:06:08.634
-Yeah.

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:10.968
-And --
-I'm pulling out jokes.

00:06:10.968 --> 00:06:12.033
From the podcast,

00:06:12.033 --> 00:06:13.200
because I work out jokes
on the podcast.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.334
-I know, but I thought
you were like,

00:06:14.334 --> 00:06:16.734
reaching for a weapon.

00:06:16.734 --> 00:06:17.968
I don't know what I thought
you were doing,

00:06:17.968 --> 00:06:19.501
but you were doing this
while I was --

00:06:19.501 --> 00:06:23.767
You were like,
"Uh-huh. Uh-huh. Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:23.767 --> 00:06:25.067
And what do you think
of it, man?"

00:06:25.067 --> 00:06:28.834
No, but I haven't
done standup in so long

00:06:28.834 --> 00:06:30.067
that I love listening to it,

00:06:30.067 --> 00:06:33.133
because you get
a great comedian on

00:06:33.133 --> 00:06:35.901
and you talk about,
you tag tags.

00:06:35.901 --> 00:06:37.300
You talk about tags.
-Sure.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.434
So if you have a great joke,
you go, "Yeah, that's good.

00:06:39.434 --> 00:06:41.400
Or you could end it this way."
-Yeah.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.701
-And then they go,
"Yeah, or it can keep it

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:45.901
the same way I just had it.:

00:06:45.901 --> 00:06:47.567
And you get kind of
in a nerdy fight,

00:06:47.567 --> 00:06:50.334
but it's funny.

00:06:50.334 --> 00:06:51.767
-Pete Holmes is one of them.
-Pete Holmes is great.

00:06:51.767 --> 00:06:54.100
Roy Wood Jr., John Mulaney.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.567
-John Mulaney is fantastic,
though.

00:06:55.567 --> 00:06:58.334
You texted me about
Aisling Bea when she was on.

00:06:58.334 --> 00:06:59.601
-She was really good.
-She was so good.

00:06:59.601 --> 00:07:01.067
-Yeah.
-I just love it.

00:07:01.067 --> 00:07:04.801
I just love how it's so
interesting, but also so funny.

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:07.133
And you go, "Oh, I miss those
days of talking to comedians

00:07:07.133 --> 00:07:09.434
and getting really into
the nuance of a joke."

00:07:09.434 --> 00:07:11.033
-Here's some jokes
I'm working out.

00:07:11.033 --> 00:07:13.434
These may or may not
make it into the show.

00:07:13.434 --> 00:07:14.767
This is how the show works.

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:17.100
I say jokes that aren't done.
-Here we go.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:18.801
-I was at a cafe in Brooklyn,

00:07:18.801 --> 00:07:21.534
and there was this woman
who said to her son,

00:07:21.534 --> 00:07:22.767
who was, like, six years old.

00:07:22.767 --> 00:07:25.801
She goes, "Arrow,
if I don't get my coffee soon,

00:07:25.801 --> 00:07:28.434
I will die, literally."

00:07:28.434 --> 00:07:30.501
And I wanted to say
to this woman, like,

00:07:30.501 --> 00:07:34.434
"Ma'am, I don't know if that's
the best way to teach your son

00:07:34.434 --> 00:07:36.434
the meaning
of the word "literally."

00:07:36.434 --> 00:07:39.234
[ Laughter ]

00:07:39.234 --> 00:07:43.934
Because if he thinks that
that's the meaning of the word,

00:07:43.934 --> 00:07:46.667
he might think
he himself is an arrow.

00:07:46.667 --> 00:07:50.834
And if he is an arrow
and he hugs you,

00:07:50.834 --> 00:07:53.067
you might die.
-Literally.

00:07:53.067 --> 00:07:54.968
Absolutely.
That was a great joke.

00:07:54.968 --> 00:07:57.033
[ Musical sting ]

00:07:57.033 --> 00:07:59.067
You might die, literally.
That's fantastic.

00:07:59.067 --> 00:08:01.167
-Oh, there we are.

00:08:01.167 --> 00:08:03.667
-Yeah, that is so good.
An arrow literally is so great.

00:08:03.667 --> 00:08:06.200
-Yeah, that's a good one.
I love that one.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:09.968
-I love that you were
able to finish it.

00:08:09.968 --> 00:08:12.033
-Maybe it is not
that well written.

00:08:12.033 --> 00:08:13.467
-You know what's funny,

00:08:13.467 --> 00:08:15.501
is our daughters
are best friends.

00:08:15.501 --> 00:08:18.634
-Our daughters are friends,
and they both love jokes.

00:08:18.634 --> 00:08:19.968
And my daughter always --

00:08:19.968 --> 00:08:25.400
One of the things about
being a comedian that's fun

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:28.968
is that you get
to make up jokes for your child.

00:08:28.968 --> 00:08:30.434
My daughter's 6,
your daughter's 8.

00:08:30.434 --> 00:08:32.033
-Yes.
-And they love it.

00:08:32.033 --> 00:08:36.067
And it's great, and you're like,
"Oh, I'm doing one thing right."

00:08:36.067 --> 00:08:37.834
-Yes. I love it.
-You know what I mean?

00:08:37.834 --> 00:08:41.767
-And so we were reading a book
the other night about penguins,

00:08:41.767 --> 00:08:43.734
and I just thought of,
I was like,

00:08:43.734 --> 00:08:46.634
"Oona, what does the penguin say

00:08:46.634 --> 00:08:48.467
to her parents
when she's hungry?"

00:08:48.467 --> 00:08:49.634
And she's like, "What"

00:08:49.634 --> 00:08:53.033
And I'm like,
"Waddle we have for dinner?"

00:08:53.033 --> 00:08:55.534
[ Musical sting ]

00:08:55.534 --> 00:08:56.834
Don't! No, please!

00:08:56.834 --> 00:09:00.067
Don't feel like
you have to laugh.

00:09:00.067 --> 00:09:02.234
It's not for you.
-Wow.

00:09:02.234 --> 00:09:04.100
-I write some jokes for you,

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:05.834
and I write some jokes
for my daughter.

00:09:05.834 --> 00:09:09.834
What you need to know as an
audience is that it killed.

00:09:09.834 --> 00:09:11.100
[ Laughter ]

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:14.133
She was like, "Haaa!
Waddle we have for dinner?"

00:09:14.133 --> 00:09:15.834
-"Waddle we have for dinner?"
How do you do it?

00:09:15.834 --> 00:09:17.434
-Mind blown.

00:09:17.434 --> 00:09:22.367
-So back to the grown-up jokes.
-Okay.

00:09:22.367 --> 00:09:24.734
-I think.
-Yes, of course.

00:09:24.734 --> 00:09:26.234
-So I'm at the cafe,

00:09:26.234 --> 00:09:29.167
and this guy walks in
and he goes,

00:09:29.167 --> 00:09:33.400
to the person, he goes,

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:37.968
"Could I have a loaf of
rosemary ciabatta?"

00:09:37.968 --> 00:09:40.367
And his 8-year-old son goes,

00:09:40.367 --> 00:09:44.601
[ Childish voice ] "Dad, I
don't want wosemary ciabatta,

00:09:44.601 --> 00:09:47.634
I want wegular ciabatta."

00:09:47.634 --> 00:09:48.901
[ Laughter ]

00:09:48.901 --> 00:09:50.868
I wanted to say to this kid,
I wanted to be like,

00:09:50.868 --> 00:09:54.400
"Life is going to
hand you all kinds of ciabatta,

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.968
but if --"
-[ Laughter ]

00:09:56.968 --> 00:10:00.767
"But if you want
wegular ciabatta,

00:10:00.767 --> 00:10:03.367
I think you've got
to hold your ground."

00:10:03.367 --> 00:10:05.667
[ Laughter ]

00:10:05.667 --> 00:10:08.868
-Life is gonna hand you
all different types of ciabatta.

00:10:08.868 --> 00:10:11.334
Oh, my God.

00:10:11.334 --> 00:10:13.934
-Oh, my gosh darn. Jeez.
-Rosemary ciabatta.

00:10:13.934 --> 00:10:15.400
[ Applause ]

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:20.000
So then people always go, like,
"You're not old," and it's true.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:21.968
It's like, I'm not old,
I'm like, middle-aged.

00:10:21.968 --> 00:10:24.501
I'm 43.
I'm halfway through my life.

00:10:24.501 --> 00:10:26.567
Not technically,
not everyone dies at 86,

00:10:26.567 --> 00:10:28.267
but no one's ever like,
"80 through 100.

00:10:28.267 --> 00:10:29.300
Those are the years."

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:30.400
-Yeah.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:32.234
-They're like, "I was 83.

00:10:32.234 --> 00:10:35.501
I reached for a grape,
and I never walked again."

00:10:35.501 --> 00:10:39.534
That's called "80 to 100."

00:10:39.534 --> 00:10:42.133
[ Applause ]

00:10:42.133 --> 00:10:43.200
-Can I choose which one?

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:44.400
-Yeah.
-I like the blue one.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.267
-Okay. [ Chuckles ]

00:10:46.267 --> 00:10:48.367
S I was in the back of an Uber,

00:10:48.367 --> 00:10:52.133
and the Uber was making a left
turn onto the Manhattan Bridge,

00:10:52.133 --> 00:10:55.167
and he hit up a pedestrian.
-What?

00:10:55.167 --> 00:10:57.167
-But it's funny
'cause she lived.

00:10:57.167 --> 00:10:58.434
[ Laughter ]

00:10:58.434 --> 00:11:01.434
She went down hard,
but you know, she was like,

00:11:01.434 --> 00:11:02.501
"Ahh!"

00:11:02.501 --> 00:11:04.667
Then she was like "I'm good!"
You know?

00:11:04.667 --> 00:11:06.968
Because New Yorkers
are resilient

00:11:06.968 --> 00:11:10.200
and often drunk.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:11.801
But it was shocking
in the back seat.

00:11:11.801 --> 00:11:12.834
I was like, "Ahh!"

00:11:12.834 --> 00:11:15.400
The second thing I thought was,

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:17.400
"one star."

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:19.367
One star.
-That's how you do it!

00:11:19.367 --> 00:11:21.968
That is how you do it
right there.

00:11:21.968 --> 00:11:25.000
That is the one.
Mike Birbiglia, everybody!

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.400
Check out Birbigs.com
for tickets

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:29.801
to "The Old Man and the Pool."

